    <name>
      <text>q2</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A shopkeeper weighed two bags of sugar using a weighing scale. The mass of the first bag was recorded as {@val1@} kg  and the mass of the second bag was recorded as {@val2@} kg.</p>

<p>Use these measurements to determine:</p>

<p>(a). The absolute error in the sum of the masses of the two bags.</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>(b). The absolute error in the difference between the masses of the two bags.</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p>(c). The relative error in the sum of the masses of the two bags.</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>

<p>(d). The relative error in the difference between the masses of the two bags.</p>
<p>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>

<p>(e). The percentage error in the sum of the masses of the two bags.</p>
<p>[[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</p>

<p>(f). The percentage error in the difference between the masses of the two bags.</p>
<p>[[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]] [[feedback:prt6]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The masses of the two bags of sugar were recorded as {@val1@} kg (to {@dp1@} decimal place(s)) and {@val2@} kg (to {@dp2@} decimal place(s)). Since each measurement is rounded to a given number of decimal places, the true mass lies within half of the smallest recorded unit on either side of the recorded value.</p>

<p>Precision of first bag = {@pre1@} kg</p>
<p>Precision of second bag = {@pre2@} kg</p>

<p>So the mass of the first bag lies between {@min1@} kg and {@max1@} kg, and the mass of the second bag lies between {@min2@} kg and {@max2@} kg.</p>

<p><strong>(a). Absolute error in the sum</strong></p>
<p>Maximum sum = {@max1@} + {@max2@} = {@maxs@} kg</p>
<p>Minimum sum = {@min1@} + {@min2@} = {@mins@} kg</p>
<p>Absolute error = \( \dfrac{{@maxs@} - {@mins@}}{2} = {@ta1@} \) kg</p>

<p><strong>(b). Absolute error in the difference</strong></p>
<p>Maximum difference = {@max1@} − {@min2@} = {@maxd@} kg</p>
<p>Minimum difference = {@min1@} − {@max2@} = {@mind@} kg</p>
<p>Absolute error = \( \dfrac{{@maxd@} - {@mind@}}{2} = {@ta2@} \) kg</p>

<p><strong>(c). Relative error in the sum</strong></p>
<p>Working sum = {@val1@} + {@val2@} = {@acts@} kg</p>
<p>Relative error = \( \dfrac{\text{absolute error}}{\text{working sum}} = \dfrac{{@ta1@}}{{@acts@}} = {@ta3@} \)</p>

<p><strong>(d). Relative error in the difference</strong></p>
<p>Working difference = {@val1@} − {@val2@} = {@actd@} kg</p>
<p>Relative error = \( \dfrac{\text{absolute error}}{\text{working difference}} = \dfrac{{@ta2@}}{{@actd@}} = {@ta4@} \)</p>

<p><strong>(e). Percentage error in the sum</strong></p>
<p>Percentage error = relative error \times 100\% = {@ta3@} \times 100\% = {@ta5@}\%</p>

<p><strong>(f). Percentage error in the difference</strong></p>
<p>Percentage error = relative error \times 100\% = {@ta4@} \times 100\% = {@ta6@}\%</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
